 ],
 "literal": "In the bustling festival of the great town / 'We will go, we will go,' you say, but on that day, here / good people, good things, in many ways, are not there. / Giving the rattle, the clapper, and leafy shoots, and saying 'These / are suitable for you,' speaking lies, / the choice beauty protected by my mother, / my lord took; we are of this state now.",
 "translation": "You say, 'We shall go, we shall go to the bustling festival in the great town.' But on that day, here, there are no good people to help us in many ways. Giving me the rattle, the bamboo clapper, and leafy shoots, and saying, 'These suit you well,' he spoke lies and took away my choice beauty that my mother had guarded so carefully. And now, this is the state we are in.",
 "modern": "பெரிய ஊரில் நடைபெறும் ஆரவாரம் மிக்க திருவிழாவிற்கு 'நாம் போவோம், போவோம்' என்று நீ சொல்கிறாய். ஆனால், அன்று அங்கே நமக்கு உதவி செய்யக்கூடிய நல்லவர்கள் பல வழிகளிலும் இருக்க மாட்டார்கள். தழல், தட்டை, தழை ஆகியவற்றைத் தந்து, 'இவை உனக்குப் பொருத்தமானவை' என்று பொய் மொழிகளைக் கூறி, என் தாய் பாதுகாத்த எனது அழகை என் தலைவன் கவர்ந்து கொண்டான். இப்போது நாம் இந்த நிலையில் இருக்கிறோம்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine (தலைவி) is speaking to her friend (தோழி) in a kuṟiñci (mountainous) landscape. The friend suggests that they go to a festival in a big town, perhaps to distract the heroine or to find a way to meet the hero. The heroine refuses, recalling how the hero first approached her when she was guarding the millet fields. He had given her playthings like the rattle (தழல்), the bamboo clapper (தட்டை), and leafy shoots (முறி), praising her beauty with sweet, false words, and eventually won her heart and took away her innocence and beauty. She points out that during the crowded festival, there will be no trustworthy people to help them, and she is now left in a state of pining, having surrendered everything to her lover.",
